analytics-service/app/serializers.py:16-18 defines the dashboard serializer as:

    def customer_response(customer: Customer) -> dict:
        """Serializes a customer for the dashboard."""
        return asdict(customer)

asdict() is a whole-object dump: it emits every field declared on the Customer dataclass at lines 7-13, which includes national_id (line 11), internal_risk_score (line 12) and support_notes (line 13) alongside the intended id/email/full_name. This is a deny-by-omission failure -- the response contract is defined by the storage model rather than by an explicit allowlist, so any field ever added to Customer (a fraud flag, an internal comment, a partner identifier) is published to the API the moment it is declared, with no code change and no review of the serializer.

The hardened sibling customer_response_public (lines 24-26) is the intended control: it projects only the PUBLIC_FIELDS tuple declared at line 21 ('id', 'email', 'full_name'), and its docstring states that a test fails when a new field appears. Its existence is evidence that customer_response is the defect.

rag-agent/config/mcp.json:6 embeds a credential literal in the MCP server definition:

     6:       "env": { "BILLING_API_KEY": "sk-acme-1f4d8e2b9c7a6d5e4f3b2a1c0d9e8f7a" }

The value has the shape of a real secret-key token (`sk-` prefix, 32 hex characters) and is committed to the repository in cleartext. The sibling entry in the same file demonstrates the intended pattern -- the `docs` server takes `"DOCS_TOKEN": "${DOCS_TOKEN}"` (line 11), an environment-variable reference resolved at launch -- so the interpolation mechanism exists and was simply not used for the more sensitive of the two credentials. The key authorises the billing MCP server, which is the same tool surface that `issue_refund` (answer.py:59-60) acts on, so it is a money-moving credential rather than a read-only one.

wire-decoder/src/ValueDecoder.java:18-26:

    public byte[] readBlob() throws IOException {
        long declared = in.readInt() & 0xFFFFFFFFL;
        if (declared >= Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
            throw new MalformedFrameException("blob too large");
        }
        byte[] blob = new byte[(int) declared];
        in.readFully(blob);
        return blob;
    }

Line 19 reads a 32-bit length straight from the peer and widens it to unsigned, so `declared` ranges over 0..4294967295. The only guard, line 20, rejects nothing below Integer.MAX_VALUE. A peer sending 0x7FFFFFFE therefore passes the check and line 23 allocates a 2,147,483,646-byte array -- roughly 2 GiB -- from an 8-byte frame prefix. The allocation at line 23 happens BEFORE readFully at line 24, so the JVM commits the memory on the peer's word alone; the connection dying at the first missing byte does not undo the commit, and the attacker never has to send the payload.

Note that the unsigned widening at line 19 makes it worse rather than safer: had the value stayed a signed int, a negative length would have thrown NegativeArraySizeException on a huge declaration; converting it to unsigned turns what would have been a crash on 0x80000000..0xFFFFFFFF into a value the >= check does catch, while leaving the entire 0..0x7FFFFFFD window allocatable. The bound that matters -- the configured one -- is never consulted: neither Settings.MAX_FRAME_BYTES (131,072) nor Settings.MAX_STRING_BYTES (65,535) appears in this method, and Settings.java:3 states these are "Limits the peer cannot change."

The hardened siblings show the intended shape. readText (lines 28-36) compares its declared length to Settings.MAX_STRING_BYTES at line 30 before allocating at line 33; FrameReader.readFrame (FrameReader.java:14-22) compares to Settings.MAX_FRAME_BYTES at line 16 before allocating at line 19 and also rejects negatives. readBlob is the one reader that allocates on an unbounded peer value.

wire-decoder/src/ValueDecoder.java:56-66 dispatches on a peer-chosen type tag:

        int tag = in.readUnsignedByte();
        switch (tag) {
            case 'S': return readText();
            case 'x': return readBlob();
            case 'M': return readMap();
            case 'A': return readList();
            case 'i': return in.readInt();

and the two container cases re-enter it: readMap (lines 38-45) calls readValue() at line 42, readList (lines 47-54) calls readValue() at line 51. The cycle readValue -> readList/readMap -> readValue is therefore driven entirely by the byte stream, and no participant carries a depth counter, a depth parameter, or a limit check. The class has no depth field at all, and Settings.java declares only MAX_FRAME_BYTES and MAX_STRING_BYTES -- there is no MAX_DEPTH for anything to consult.

The nesting is extremely cheap for the attacker: an empty-but-continuing list costs 3 bytes per level (the 'A' tag plus a 2-byte entry count of 1), so a frame that FrameReader.readFrame happily accepts at the MAX_FRAME_BYTES ceiling of 131,072 bytes (FrameReader.java:16) encodes on the order of 43,000 nesting levels -- one to two orders of magnitude beyond a default JVM thread stack. The byte-length limit is not a depth limit, which is precisely why the frame check does not contain this.

The failure mode is StackOverflowError, a java.lang.Error rather than the MalformedFrameException the decoder raises for every other malformed input, so it escapes the protocol's own error path: handlers that catch IOException (the parent of MalformedFrameException, FrameReader.java:14) will not catch it, and the partially built HashMap/ArrayList are abandoned mid-construction.  [pipeline note] Conditionally viable: Fully triggerable as written, but a StackOverflowError in a per-connection decode thread may be contained by the host application's thread-level error handling, which is not in this corpus.

certs-authz/api/resources.py:29-35:

    class CertificateTags(AuthenticatedResource):
        def post(self, certificate_id, data=None):
            for tag in data["tags"]:
                permission = TagPermission(tag.id, service.current_user())
                if not permission.can():
                    return dict(message="You are not authorized to use this tag"), 403
            return service.attach_tags(certificate_id, data["tags"])

The loop authorises one half of the operation -- the tags -- via TagPermission.can() (permissions.py:20-21). The other half, the object being mutated, is never authorised: `certificate_id` arrives from the URL path and is passed to service.attach_tags at line 35 without any owner check, without an AuthorityPermission, and without being resolved through an owner-scoped lookup. The module offers exactly two permission objects (permissions.py) and neither is applied to the certificate here.

This is the object-level gap the threat model calls out: the AuthenticatedResource base class (resources.py:7-10) only proves a session exists -- its own docstring says "It does not imply any role" -- and the sibling read path CertificatesList.get (line 15) shows the module does scope by principal when it means to (`service.list_for(service.current_user())`). The write path here does not.

Note on confidence: service.attach_tags is not present in this corpus (certs-authz ships only models.py, permissions.py, resources.py and schemas.py), so it cannot be ruled out that the scoping happens one layer down. What is verifiable is that the resource layer -- where every other authorisation decision in this module is taken, including the tag check three lines above -- performs none for the certificate.  [pipeline note] Grounded: CertificateTags.post (resources.py:29-35) validates each tag against the caller's own tags but passes the path-supplied certificate_id to service.attach_tags with no ownership check. service.attach_tags is not present in this corpus, so a scoping check one layer down cannot be excluded. The asymmetry with its own tag check is real evidence, so this is not dismissed; but the sink is unreadable here.
